 --------------------------
 #6 - The Right and the Wrong Models for Evaluating Callable Municipal Bonds
 --------------------------
 6.1) Introduction
	- Redemption features => call options purchased by the issuer at the inception of a bond transaction.
		- Funded through a re-financing issue called a re-funding.
	- Two Types of Refunding => Current and advance
	- Advance Refunding => Occurs more than 90 days in advance of the call date
		- Proceeds from the refunding issue to purchase a portfolio of eligible securities
			– State and Local Government Securities SLGS, US Treasuries, or Agency securities
		- Match cash flows of the refunded bonds to their respective first optional redemption dates
	- Ang, Green, and Xing (2013) => Advance Refunding provides short-term budget relief, but destroys value to the issuer
		- Pre-commitment to call => issuer surrenders the option not to call should interest rates rise before the call date
	- Kalotay, Yang, and Fabozzi (2007) => Advance Refunding Option – ARO – intimately tied to call option
		- ARO measured incrementally above that of the call option
	- Optimal Exercise => Selecting when to Refund bonds
	- Refunding Heuristics => PV interest savings breach 2% and 6% threshold
 6.2) Types of Term-structure Models and their Roles - Deterministic
	- Display zero volatility - Used for VRDOs
 6.3) Pricing Models – Problems Specific to the Municipal Market
	- High-to-low Interest Refunding => Interest cost saving through financing with lower interest rates
	- Other reasons (not considered here):
		- Retiring bonds with restrictive covenants
		- Applying excess revenues to shrink the balance sheet
		- Replacing insured bonds
	- Ability of Issuers to Arbitrage: Municipal issuers aannot arbitrage the mispricing of market discount bonds.
		- IRC §148 specifically prohibits arbitrage across municipal bonds and other types of bonds, for example, treasury and corporate bonds
			- By tax-exempt institutions
	- Shorting Municipal Bonds: Unlike treasuries, shorting municipal bonds hard - only tax-exempt authorities/institutions pay tax-exempt interest
		- Investor lending a municipal bond to a dealer receives taxable dividend
			- Because that dividend is paid by a dealer, not a tax-exempt institution
		- Hard to locate a suitable municipal bond as a hedge because of the sheer number of municipal securities
		- Shorting related interest rate securities, eg treasuries, corporate bonds, opens up potentially large basis risk
		- Trading costs may be much higher than the treasuries
	- Quantitative Municipal Hedge Strategy Inputs
		- #1: Option-free tax-exempt yields for the issuer
		- #2: Implied volatilities for the yields in market input #1
		- #3: Market yield curve for escrow securities – SGLS or other
		- #4: Implied volatilities for escrow yields in market input #3
		- #5: Option free taxable yields for the issuer – for callable, non-advance refundable bonds
		- #6: Implied volatilities for the taxable yield curve in market input #5
		- #7: Implied correlations between option-free tax-exempt yields and the escrow yields
		- #8: Implied correlations between option-free taxable yields and escrow yields
		- #9: Implied correlations between tax-exempt and taxable issuer yields
	- Implying Volatilities for Escrow Yields => SLGS are published at the end of each trading day on the treasury’s website
		- SLGS are designed to be 1 bp lower than treasuries, one might imply market input #4 from the prices of traded treasury options
	- Estimating Taxable and Tax-free Yields
		- #1 and #5 using secondary market data and a methodology like Nelson and Siegel
		- No market implied volatilities or correlations
 6.4) Characteristics of a Good Model for Analyzing Municipal Bond Call Features
	- Positive Nominal Rates
	- Full Term Structure Dynamics: Including volatilities and correlations for tax-exempt, taxable, and escrow instruments.
	- Control on Yield Curve Shapes: While allowing for variation of slopes, bumps, and twists
	- Recovering Historical Yield Curve Change Distributions
	- Replication of Historical Yield Correlations: Both within and across Markets
	- Distribution Tails for the Yields: Low high end and fat low end
	- Use the above to analyze and evaluate different exercise criteria, and establish refunding criteria
 6.4) Expected Present Value – EPV – Savings
	- Simulate taxable issuer yield curve, tax-free issuer yield curve, escrow yeld curve
	- Quarterly Refunding of Candidate Bonds => value of refunding bonds assuming one percent for costs of bond issuance
	- Negative Arbitrage => Escrow Yield < Refuncing Yield
	- NPV savings calculation reflects negative arbitrage where it exists
		- Ensures escrow yield is no greater than the bond yield for tax-exempt re-fundings
		- 5% NPV savings threshold - refunding time
			- Bonds with 90 day call notice
			- Taxable vs. tax-free refunding
			- Optimal exercise as a function of rate and time
